Sounds good to me.

> How significant is the runtime overhead?

The concern with sorting necessitates refactoring the runtime dispatch to enable dispatching only during the load of Numpy, similar to argmax and argmin. Refer to: [link](https://github.com/numpy/numpy/blob/7b56da8c46427d487b9337780417a9aad64eb295/numpy/core/src/multiarray/arraytypes.c.src#L4595-L4614).

However, this can be handled via C++ static initialization to guarantee only one call, which may affect small arrays. See: [link](https://github.com/numpy/numpy/pull/24384#discussion_r1292330069).

In general, it definitely impacts the speed of loading the numpy module. It makes several Python API calls to insert an entry into the global dict __cpu_targets_info__ for each runtime dispatch. Refer to: [link](https://github.com/numpy/numpy/blob/b4361f088cd90e52c7cec46404bc0d28c0e92d55/numpy/core/src/common/npy_cpu_dispatch.c#L29-L75).","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/numpy/numpy/issues/comments/1681000643/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null}}

